= Section I
{parent=Paper 1}

= 1I
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1I}

Use the <reduction algorithm for a positive definite binary quadratic form>. A determinant-one shear $(x,y)\mapsto(x+ky,y)$ puts the middle coefficient in $[-a,a]$. If the resulting last coefficient is smaller than $a$, the determinant-one substitution $(x,y)\mapsto(-y,x)$ decreases the positive integer leading coefficient. Repeating terminates with a <reduced positive definite binary quadratic form> satisfying $|b|\leq a\leq c$. These substitutions preserve <proper equivalence of binary quadratic forms> and the <discriminant of a binary quadratic form>.

The <enumeration of reduced binary quadratic forms> gives
$$
163=4ac-b^2\geq3a^2,\qquad1\leq a\leq7.
$$
Since $b^2\equiv-163\equiv1\pmod4$, $b$ is odd. For $|b|=1,3,5,7$, the possible values of $(b^2+163)/4$ are respectively $41,43,47,53$. Each is a <prime number> greater than seven. But $ac=(b^2+163)/4$, so its positive divisor $a\leq7$ must be one. Then $|b|\leq a$ forces $b=\pm1$ and $c=41$. The two signs are properly equivalent: substituting $x\mapsto x-y$ in $x^2+xy+41y^2$ gives $x^2-xy+41y^2$. Thus every form in question is properly equivalent to
$$
\boxed{x^2+xy+41y^2.}
$$

= 2F
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= i
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The closed-set version of the <Baire category theorem> says that if a nonempty <complete metric space> $X$ is a countable union $X=\bigcup_{n=1}^\infty F_n$ of <closed sets>, then at least one $F_n$ has nonempty <interior>. Equivalently, a nonempty <complete metric space> cannot be a countable union of closed <nowhere dense sets>.

= ii
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For each integer $n\geq0$, put $Z_n=\{z:f^{(n)}(z)=0\}$. Every <derivative> $f^{(n)}$ is an <entire function> and cannot be identically zero: otherwise repeated integration would make $f$ a <polynomial>. By the <identity theorem for holomorphic functions>, its <zero set> has empty <interior>, and <continuity> makes $Z_n$ a <closed set>. Thus $Z_n$ is <nowhere dense>.

The complex plane with its usual <metric> is a <complete metric space>, so the <Baire category theorem> gives $z_0\notin\bigcup_{n\geq0}Z_n$. The <Taylor series> there has coefficients
$$
\boxed{\frac{f^{(n)}(z_0)}{n!}\ne0\quad\text{for every }n\geq0.}
$$

= 3G
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry and Groups}

= Solution
{parent=3G}

Each nonidentity element of the <special orthogonal group> $\operatorname{SO}(3)$ is a rotation fixing exactly the two unit vectors along its axis. Since $G$ is a <finite group>, $\Omega$ is finite. If $v\in\Omega$ is fixed by $g\ne1$ and $h\in G$, then $hv$ is fixed by $hgh^{-1}\ne1$. Hence $G$ acts on $\Omega$ by a <group action>.

Let $N=|G|>1$, let $r$ be the number of <orbits of a group action>, and choose representatives with <stabilizer subgroups> of orders $n_1,\ldots,n_r$, each at least two. Count pairs $(g,v)$ with $g\ne1$ and $gv=v$. Counting by $g$ gives $2(N-1)$. Counting by <group orbits> and using the <orbit-stabilizer theorem> gives
$$
\sum_{j=1}^r\frac N{n_j}(n_j-1)=2(N-1),\qquad
\sum_{j=1}^r\left(1-\frac1{n_j}\right)=2-\frac2N<2.
$$
Every summand is at least $1/2$, so $r<4$: **there are at most three orbits**. For the trivial <group>, $\Omega$ is empty and the assertion also holds.

For the rotation group of a regular <dodecahedron>, $N=60$. The three <group orbits> are the unit vectors towards the 12 face centres, 20 vertices and 30 edge midpoints, whose <stabilizer subgroup> orders are respectively $5,3,2$. Rotations about these axes exhaust the nonidentity rotations. Consequently the orbit sizes are
$$
\boxed{12,\quad20,\quad30.}
$$

= 4G
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=4G}

Use <binary search>: maintain the interval of possible integers and ask whether $n$ is at least the first integer in its upper half. Each answer reduces the number of possibilities to at most its previous number divided by two, rounded up. After $q$ questions at most $\lceil10^6/2^q\rceil$ possibilities remain. Since $2^{20}=1\,048\,576>10^6$, **twenty questions suffice**.

A yes/no <decision tree> of depth twenty has at most $2^{20}$ leaves, whereas the possible finishing orders number $15!=1\,307\,674\,368\,000$. Thus **twenty questions cannot identify the finishing order of fifteen horses**, assuming no ties. With unrestricted yes/no questions, the exact minimum for $n$ horses is $\lceil\log_2(n!)\rceil$: the leaf count is a lower bound, and repeatedly partitioning the remaining <permutations> into two nearly equal sets attains it. In particular fifteen horses require 41 questions.

By <Stirling's formula>,
$$
\boxed{\log_2(n!)=n\log_2n-(\log_2e)n+\frac12\log_2(2\pi n)+O(n^{-1}),}
$$
so the leading number of questions is $n\log_2n$. These questions may ask about whole sets of possible orders; restricting them to pairwise comparisons is a different model.

= 5J
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=5J}

Write $u(y,\theta)=\partial_\theta\log f(y,\theta)$ for the single-observation <score function>. Differentiating the normalization of the <probability density function> gives
$$
\mathbb E_\theta[u(Y,\theta)]
=\int f(y,\theta)\partial_\theta\log f(y,\theta)\,dy
=\partial_\theta\int f(y,\theta)\,dy=0.
$$
For discrete observations, use a sum instead. The allowed interchange of differentiation and integration is the usual regular-model assumption with common support. Adding these identities gives $\mathbb E_\theta U_n=0$.

Let $p_\theta$ be the joint density. Because $T$ is an <unbiased estimator> with no explicit dependence on the parameter, differentiating $\mathbb E_\theta T=\theta$ gives
$$
1=\int T\,\partial_\theta p_\theta
=\mathbb E_\theta[TU_n]
=\operatorname{Cov}_\theta(T,U_n).
$$
The scores are <independent random variables> with mean zero, so <variance additivity for independent random variables> gives $\operatorname{Var}_\theta(U_n)=n\operatorname{Var}_\theta(U_1)$. The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> applied to the centred estimator and the score now gives the <Cramér-Rao lower bound>:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}_\theta(T)\geq\frac1{n\operatorname{Var}_\theta(U_1)}.}
$$
The denominator is the sample <Fisher information>. This bound concerns regular models with positive finite information; the preceding covariance identity explains why a small estimator variance requires a sufficiently informative score.

= 6B
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=6B}

The equation is a <conservation law> $n_t+J_x=0$ with <diffusive flux> $J=-D(n_0/n)n_x$. For positive density, insects move down the density gradient, with effective <diffusion coefficient> $Dn_0/n$: dispersal is stronger in low-density regions. This is <logarithmic diffusion>, since the equation is $n_t=Dn_0(\log n)_{xx}$.

Put $\tau=Dt$ and $\xi=x/\tau^\beta$. A <self-similar solution> $n=n_0\tau^{-\beta}g(\xi)$ conserves total number when $\int_{\mathbb R}g=1$. Its width shrinks to zero as $t\downarrow0$; for an integrable nonnegative $g$, changing variables in the integral against a bounded <continuous function> proves convergence to $n_0$ times the <Dirac delta distribution> at the origin.

Substitution gives
$$
-\beta\tau^{-\beta-1}(g+\xi g')
=\tau^{-2\beta}(g'/g)'.
$$
Matching powers for a nonstationary profile forces **$\beta=1$**. The profile equation integrates to $g'/g=-\xi g+C$. For a centred even profile, $C=0$, and integration of $g'=-\xi g^2$ gives $g=2/(\xi^2+a^2)$. Normalization requires $2\pi/a=1$, hence
$$
\boxed{g(\xi)=\frac2{\xi^2+4\pi^2},\qquad
n(x,t)=\frac{2n_0Dt}{x^2+4\pi^2D^2t^2}.}
$$
This is $n_0$ times a <Cauchy distribution> density with scale $2\pi Dt$. It is positive, has total mass $n_0$, and directly satisfies the <nonlinear diffusion equation> for $t>0$.

= 7C
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= Solution
{parent=7C}

The <fixed points> are $(0,0)$ and $(\pm|\mu|,\mu^2)$. The <Jacobian matrix> is
$$
J(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}\mu^2-y&-x\\2x&-1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At the origin its <eigenvalues> are $\mu^2$ and $-1$, so it is a <saddle equilibrium> for every allowed $\mu$. At either nonzero fixed point the <characteristic polynomial> is $\lambda^2+\lambda+2\mu^2$, giving
$$
\boxed{\lambda_\pm=\frac{-1\pm\sqrt{1-8\mu^2}}2.}
$$
Thus both are <stable nodes> for $0<|\mu|<1/(2\sqrt2)$ and <stable focuses> for $|\mu|>1/(2\sqrt2)$. At equality the repeated <eigenvalue> is $-1/2$ and the <matrix> is not scalar, so there is only one independent <eigenvector>: the fixed point is a stable improper node.

The <stable manifold> of the origin is **$x=0$**, exactly: there $\dot y=-y$. Conversely, a trajectory converging to the origin with $x\ne0$ would eventually have $\dot x/x=\mu^2-y>\mu^2/2$, which precludes convergence of $x$ to zero.

For the local <unstable manifold>, write $y=h(x)=ax^2+bx^4+O(x^6)$. Reflection symmetry makes $h$ even, and the <invariance equation for a graph> requires
$$
h'(x)x(\mu^2-h(x))=-h(x)+x^2.
$$
Comparing quadratic and quartic terms gives $2a\mu^2=1-a$ and $4b\mu^2-2a^2=-b$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y=\frac{x^2}{1+2\mu^2}+\frac{2x^4}{(1+4\mu^2)(1+2\mu^2)^2}+O(x^6).}
$$

= 8E
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=8E}

At $x=0$, both denominators are nonzero because $a\notin2\pi\mathbb Z$; they remain nonzero for every real $x\geq0$. The integrand is therefore bounded on each finite interval. At infinity its terms decay respectively as $O(e^{-(1+b)x})$ and $O(e^{-(1-b)x})$, so $0<b<1$ gives <absolute convergence>.

Set $t=e^{-x}$. The two integrals become
$$
I(a,b)=e^{-ia}\int_0^1\frac{t^b}{1-e^{-ia}t}\,dt
-e^{ia}\int_0^1\frac{t^{-b}}{1-e^{ia}t}\,dt.
$$
By the <Euler integral for the hypergeometric function> and the <Gamma function recurrence>, $\int_0^1t^{s-1}/(1-zt)\,dt={}_2F_1(1,s;s+1;z)/s$ for $s>0$ and the indicated unit-circle values $z\ne1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{I(a,b)=\frac{e^{-ia}}{1+b}{}_2F_1(1,1+b;2+b;e^{-ia})
-\frac{e^{ia}}{1-b}{}_2F_1(1,1-b;2-b;e^{ia}).}
$$
The <Gauss hypergeometric functions> here use their continuation from the Euler integral, so no pole crosses the integration interval.

= 9C
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= i
{parent=9c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <canonical momentum> is $p_i=\partial L/\partial\dot x_i=m\dot x_i+qA_i$. Its time derivative contains the total derivative along the trajectory: $\dot p_i=m\ddot x_i+q\partial_tA_i+q\dot x_j\partial_jA_i$. The <Euler-Lagrange equation> therefore gives
$$
m\ddot x_i=q\left[-\partial_i\phi-\partial_tA_i+
\dot x_j(\partial_iA_j-\partial_jA_i)\right].
$$
The <magnetic field> is $\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A$, so the last term is $(\dot{\mathbf x}\times\mathbf B)_i$. With the <electric field> $\mathbf E=-\nabla\phi-\partial_t\mathbf A$, this is precisely the <Lorentz force law>:
$$
\boxed{m\ddot{\mathbf x}=q(\mathbf E+\dot{\mathbf x}\times\mathbf B).}
$$

= ii
{parent=9c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The specified <vector potential> gives $\mathbf B=(0,0,B)$. Put $\omega=qB/m$, retaining its sign. The <Lorentz force law> yields $\ddot x=\omega\dot y$, $\ddot y=-\omega\dot x$ and $\ddot z=0$. For initial velocities $(u,v,w)$ and $\omega\ne0$, integration gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
x(t)&=x_0+\frac u\omega\sin\omega t+\frac v\omega(1-\cos\omega t),\\
y(t)&=y_0+\frac v\omega\sin\omega t-\frac u\omega(1-\cos\omega t),\\
z(t)&=z_0+wt.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The transverse motion is a circle centred at $(x_0+v/\omega,y_0-u/\omega)$ with radius $\sqrt{u^2+v^2}/|\omega|$. Thus the full orbit is a <helix> around a line parallel to the <magnetic field>, with period $2\pi/|\omega|$ and signed axial advance $2\pi w/|\omega|$ per turn. The charge sign determines the sense of rotation. If $w=0$ it is a circle, if $u=v=0$ it is a straight line parallel to the field, and if $qB=0$ all motion is uniform rectilinear motion.

= 10E
{parent=Section I}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= Solution
{parent=10E}

For neighbouring <comoving observers> separated by a small physical distance $d\ell$, the recession velocity is $H(t)d\ell$, where $H=\dot a/a$ is the <Hubble parameter>. During the photon's travel time $dt=d\ell/c$, the local <Doppler effect> gives $d\lambda/\lambda=Hdt$. Integrating along the ray gives the <cosmological wavelength-redshift relation>:
$$
\log\frac{\lambda_0}{\lambda_e}=\int_{t_e}^{t_0}\frac{\dot a}a\,dt,
\qquad\boxed{1+z=\frac{a(t_0)}{a(t_e)}.}
$$
The same ratio stretches the arrival interval of successive wave crests and of successive emitted pulses: $dt_0=(1+z)dt_e$. Each <photon> loses a factor $1+z$ in energy because $E=hc/\lambda$. Thus energy arriving through the sphere per unit observer time has one factor from <cosmological redshift> and one from time dilation:
$$
\boxed{\frac{dE_0}{dt_0}=\frac L{(1+z)^2}.}
$$
This is the total power crossing the sphere; division by its area would give the observed <flux>.

= Section II
{parent=Paper 1}

= 11G
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry and Groups}

= Solution
{parent=11G}

Identify the <Möbius group> with $\operatorname{PSL}_2(\mathbb C)$, the orientation-preserving <isometry group> of three-dimensional <hyperbolic space>. Here a <properly discontinuous group action> permits finite stabilizers: for each compact set $K$, only finitely many $g$ satisfy $gK\cap K\ne\varnothing$.

Fix $o\in\mathbb H^3$. The set of isometries with $d(o,go)\leq R$ is compact. To see this, an orientation-preserving isometry is uniquely determined by the image of an oriented orthonormal frame at $o$. The image point lies in a compact closed hyperbolic ball and its frame lies in a compact rotation-group fiber. These choices parametrize the indicated set of isometries continuously.

A <discrete subgroup> of a <topological group> is closed: otherwise distinct subgroup elements accumulating at the same point would have quotients tending to the identity, contradicting isolation of the identity. Its intersection with a compact set is therefore finite. If $K\subseteq B(o,R)$ and $gK\cap K\ne\varnothing$, choosing $x,gx\in K$ gives $d(o,go)\leq d(o,gx)+d(gx,go)\leq2R$. Hence discreteness implies a <properly discontinuous group action>. Conversely, if the subgroup is not discrete, distinct $g_n$ tend to the identity. For a compact ball $K$ with $o$ in its interior, eventually $g_no\in K$, so infinitely many $g_nK$ meet $K$, contradicting proper discontinuity. This proves the equivalence.

Matrices of determinant one over the <Gaussian integers> are closed under multiplication and inversion, with inverse $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}d&-b\\-c&a\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Quotienting by $\{\pm I\}$ gives the specified group of <Möbius transformations>. The Gaussian integers form a <discrete set> in the complex plane, so these matrices form a <discrete subgroup> of $\operatorname{SL}_2(\mathbb C)$, and the finite quotient remains discrete. Thus this group acts properly discontinuously on $\mathbb H^3$.

Explicit representatives of all four types are
$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{type}&\text{matrix}&\text{trace}\\\hline
\text{elliptic}&\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}&0\\
\text{parabolic}&\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}&2\\
\text{hyperbolic}&\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}&3\\
\text{loxodromic}&\begin{pmatrix}1+i&1\\i&1\end{pmatrix}&2+i
\end{array}}
$$
The first is $z\mapsto-1/z$ and has order two; the second is the nonidentity translation $z\mapsto z+1$. The third has positive real reciprocal <eigenvalues> $(3\pm\sqrt5)/2$, giving translation along an axis without rotation. For the last, a nonreal trace excludes both unit-modulus and real eigenvalues. Its multiplier therefore has modulus different from one and a nonzero rotation angle: it is a <loxodromic Möbius transformation>.

