= 15E
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= i
{parent=15e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Differentiate the <Friedmann equation> and use cosmological energy conservation:
$$
2H\dot H-2\frac{kc^2}{a^2}H
=\frac{8\pi G}{3}\dot\rho
=-8\pi GH(\rho+P/c^2).
$$
For an expanding universe $H>0$, division by $2H$ and substitution of the <Friedmann equation> give the <Friedmann acceleration equation>
$$
\boxed{\frac{\ddot a}a=\dot H+H^2=-\frac{4\pi G}{3}(\rho+3P/c^2).}
$$
The <strong energy condition> makes $\ddot a\leq0$. Since $aH=\dot a$ and $\dot a>0$,
$$
\frac d{dt}(aH)^2=2\dot a\ddot a\leq0.
$$
Thus the quantity is nonincreasing, with strict decrease when the energy condition is strict. Dividing the <Friedmann equation> by $H^2$ yields
$$
\boxed{\Omega-1=\frac{kc^2}{(aH)^2}.}
$$
For fixed nonzero curvature, the magnitude of $\Omega-1$ grows as expansion decelerates. The observed closeness to one therefore requires much tighter early-time closeness to one in the standard decelerating model: this is the <flatness problem>. Accelerated expansion during inflation reverses this evolution and suppresses the curvature contribution.

= ii
{parent=15e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Normalize the <scale factor> by $a(t_0)=1$. Separate conservation for a constant equation-of-state parameter $w$ gives $d\log\rho_i=-3(1+w_i)d\log a$. Thus radiation has $\rho_R=\rho_{R0}a^{-4}$ while the <dark energy> component with $w=-1$ has constant density. Flatness gives $\Omega_{R0}+\Omega_{\Lambda0}=1$, so
$$
\boxed{\rho(t)=\frac{3H_0^2}{8\pi G}
\left(\frac{\Omega_{R0}}{a^4}+\Omega_{\Lambda0}\right)
=\frac{3H_0^2}{8\pi G}\frac{\Omega_{R0}}{a^4}
\left[1+\frac{1-\Omega_{R0}}{\Omega_{R0}}a^4\right].}
$$
Here $\rho$ has mass-density units in the stated <Friedmann equation>; the corresponding energy density is $\rho c^2$.

Assume both density parameters are positive and choose the Big Bang at $t=0$. The expanding branch satisfies $\dot a=H_0\sqrt{\Omega_{R0}a^{-2}+\Omega_{\Lambda0}a^2}$. Set $u=a^2$ and integrate:
$$
\dot u=2H_0\sqrt{\Omega_{R0}+\Omega_{\Lambda0}u^2},\qquad
\operatorname{arsinh}\left(u\sqrt{\frac{\Omega_{\Lambda0}}{\Omega_{R0}}}\right)
=2H_0\sqrt{\Omega_{\Lambda0}}\,t.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{a(t)=\left(\frac{\Omega_{R0}}{\Omega_{\Lambda0}}\right)^{1/4}
\left[\sinh(2H_0\sqrt{\Omega_{\Lambda0}}\,t)\right]^{1/2},\quad
\alpha=\left(\frac{\Omega_{R0}}{\Omega_{\Lambda0}}\right)^{1/4},\quad
\beta=2H_0\sqrt{\Omega_{\Lambda0}}.}
$$
The factor $H_0$ is required because $\beta$ has inverse-time units. As $t\downarrow0$, $a(t)\sim(2H_0\sqrt{\Omega_{R0}}\,t)^{1/2}$, the expected radiation-dominated power law. As $t\to\infty$, $a(t)\sim\alpha e^{H_0\sqrt{\Omega_{\Lambda0}}t}/\sqrt2$, the exponential expansion driven by a <cosmological constant>.

= 16H
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

= Solution
{parent=16H}

The inductive definition of <ordinal addition> is $\alpha+0=\alpha$, $\alpha+(\beta+1)=(\alpha+\beta)+1$, and $\alpha+\lambda=\sup_{\beta<\lambda}(\alpha+\beta)$ for a nonzero limit ordinal $\lambda$. The synthetic definition is the <order type> of the <order sum> obtained by putting a copy of $\beta$ after a disjoint copy of $\alpha$.

These agree by <transfinite induction> on the second argument. Appending no points gives $\alpha$; appending one new greatest point gives a successor. At a limit stage, the order sum is exhausted by the increasing initial segments obtained by appending each $\beta<\lambda$, so its order type is their supremum. Thus the synthetic construction satisfies all three recursion clauses, and uniqueness of transfinite recursion proves equivalence.

= i
{parent=16h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** By the successor and limit clauses for <ordinal multiplication>,
$$
\boxed{2\omega=\sup_{n<\omega}2n=\omega,\qquad
\omega2=\omega+\omega>\omega.}
$$
Multiplication is not commutative because it counts ordered copies, rather than merely the cardinality of their union.

= ii
{parent=16h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**Always true.** In the order-sum interpretation of <ordinal multiplication>, $\alpha(\beta+\gamma)$ consists of copies of $\alpha$ indexed first by $\beta$ and then by $\gamma$. The first portion has <order type> $\alpha\beta$ and the second $\alpha\gamma$, proving $\alpha(\beta+\gamma)=\alpha\beta+\alpha\gamma$. Equivalently, <transfinite induction> on $\gamma$ uses the successor rule, associativity of <ordinal addition>, and continuity of both operations in the right argument at limit ordinals.

= iii
{parent=16h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**False.** Take $\alpha=\omega^2+1$, which satisfies the proposed lower bound. Associativity of <ordinal addition> and $1+\omega^2=\omega^2$ give
$$
\boxed{(\omega^2+1)+\omega^2=\omega^2\cdot2,\qquad
\omega^2+(\omega^2+1)=\omega^2\cdot2+1.}
$$
The final successor survives only in the second expression.

= iv
{parent=16h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**False.** Take $\alpha=\omega_1+1$. Continuity of <ordinal multiplication> in the right argument gives $(\omega_1+1)\omega_1=\omega_1^2$. For a precise bound, for each countable ordinal $\beta$ monotonicity gives
$$
\omega_1\beta\leq(\omega_1+1)\beta\leq(\omega_1\cdot2)\beta
=\omega_1(2\beta).
$$
Both bounding expressions have supremum $\omega_1^2$ as $\beta$ ranges below $\omega_1$, because $2\beta$ is still countable and these indices are cofinal. But the successor rule gives
$$
\boxed{\alpha\omega_1=\omega_1^2,\qquad
\omega_1\alpha=\omega_1^2+\omega_1>\omega_1^2.}
$$

= 17F
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= Solution
{parent=17F}

A <matching in a graph> is a set of edges with no shared endpoints. A matching from $X$ to $Y$ here covers every vertex of $X$. Necessity of the <Hall marriage theorem> condition is immediate: the partners of vertices of $A\subseteq X$ are distinct members of its neighbour set $\Gamma(A)$.

For sufficiency, take a matching of maximum size. If it leaves $x\in X$ unmatched, follow alternating unmatched and matched edges from $x$. Let $A\subseteq X$ and $B\subseteq Y$ be the reachable vertices. No vertex of $B$ can be unmatched, because the resulting <augmenting path in a matching> would increase the matching. Every neighbour of $A$ is in $B$, and the matching pairs $B$ bijectively with $A\setminus\{x\}$. Thus $|\Gamma(A)|=|B|=|A|-1$, contradicting Hall's condition. The maximum matching therefore covers $X$.

For the deficiency bound, adjoin $d$ new vertices to $Y$, each adjacent to all of $X$. Every nonempty $A$ then has $|\Gamma(A)|+d\geq|A|$ neighbours. Apply the <Hall marriage theorem> and remove the at most $d$ edges using new vertices. This leaves **at least $|X|-d$ independent edges**. The lower bound is vacuous if $d>|X|$.

Write the <adjacency matrix of a graph> in bipartite block form $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&C\\C^T&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. If Hall's condition failed for $A\subseteq X$, the linear map from vectors supported on $A$ to their neighbour coordinates in $Y$ would have fewer output than input coordinates. It therefore has a nonzero <kernel>, giving a nonzero vector $v$ supported on $A$ with $C^Tv=0$. The vector $(v,0)$ would be a zero <eigenvector> of the adjacency matrix. Consequently **absence of the zero eigenvalue implies a matching covering $X$**.

The converse is **false**, even with equal part sizes. The four-cycle $K_{2,2}$ has a <perfect matching>, but its matrix $C=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ is singular. Its adjacency matrix has eigenvalues $2,-2,0,0$.

= 18H
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= i
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <field homomorphism> fixing $K$ is injective, since its kernel is an ideal of a field and cannot contain one. It is also $K$-linear. Equal finite <field extension degrees> then make it a bijection, hence a $K$-isomorphism.

Equal degrees alone do not ensure that any such homomorphism exists. For example $\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)$ and $\mathbb Q(\sqrt3)$ both have degree two, but a $\mathbb Q$-isomorphism would require a square root of two in $\mathbb Q(\sqrt3)$. Squaring $a+b\sqrt3$ with rational $a,b$ would give $2ab=0$ and $a^2+3b^2=2$. Neither $a^2=2$ nor $b^2=2/3$ has a rational solution. Thus **the two degree-two extensions are not isomorphic over $\mathbb Q$**.

= ii
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

An isomorphism fixing $L$ fixes its subfield $K$, so **an $L$-isomorphism is a $K$-isomorphism**. The converse is false because an isomorphism over $K$ may act nontrivially on $L$.

Take $K=\mathbb Q$, $L=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)$, $\alpha=2^{1/4}>0$, $F=L(\alpha)$ and $F'=L(i\alpha)$. Both are degree-two extensions of $L$. The polynomial $X^4-2$ is irreducible over $\mathbb Q$ by the <Eisenstein criterion>, and both $\alpha$ and $i\alpha$ are roots. Sending $\alpha$ to $i\alpha$ therefore gives a $\mathbb Q$-isomorphism $F\to F'$; on $L$ it sends $\sqrt2$ to $-\sqrt2$.

There is no $L$-isomorphism. The two <quadratic extensions> adjoin square roots of $\sqrt2$ and $-\sqrt2$. Their square classes would agree only if $-1$ were a square in $L$. To check this criterion directly, a square root of $\sqrt2$ in $L(i\alpha)$ would have form $a+b(i\alpha)$; its square forces $ab=0$. The case $b=0$ would put $\alpha$ in $L$, while $a=0$ would require $b^2=-1$. Both are impossible, the latter since $L\subseteq\mathbb R$. Thus **the extensions are isomorphic over $K$ but not over $L$**.

= 19I
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= i
{parent=19i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

If $g$ belongs to the <centre of a group>, then $\rho(g)$ commutes with $\rho(h)$ for every $h\in G$. The <Schur lemma> says that an endomorphism of a finite-dimensional complex <irreducible representation> commuting with the representation is scalar. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\rho(g)=\lambda_gI\quad(g\in Z).}
$$
Since $g$ has finite order, $\lambda_g$ is a <root of unity> and has modulus one.

= ii
{parent=19i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $\chi$ be the <character of a representation> of $\rho$. The <character orthogonality> identity for an <irreducible character> is $\sum_{g\in G}|\chi(g)|^2=|G|=n$. For $g\in Z$, the scalar formula from part (i) gives $|\chi(g)|=d$. Keeping these $m$ nonnegative summands yields
$$
\boxed{md^2\leq n,\qquad d^2\leq n/m.}
$$

= iii
{parent=19i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If $\rho$ is a <faithful representation>, $g\mapsto\lambda_g$ embeds $Z$ into the multiplicative group of complex numbers. Its image is a finite group of <roots of unity>, hence lies in the cyclic group of $n$th roots of unity and is itself cyclic. Thus **$Z$ is cyclic**.

= Solution
{parent=19I}

For the order-18 group, the index-two subgroup $P$ is normal and $G=\langle P,t\rangle$. Restrict a complex <irreducible representation> $V$ to the finite abelian group $P$. Simultaneous diagonalization decomposes it into character spaces $V_\chi$. The inversion relation makes $t$ exchange $V_\chi$ and $V_{\chi^{-1}}$. The sum of spaces belonging to any such character orbit is $G$-invariant, so irreducibility leaves only one orbit.

If the character is trivial, all of $P$ is in the representation's kernel. Otherwise $\chi$ has order three and $\chi\ne\chi^{-1}$. The only spaces are $V_\chi$ and $V_{\chi^{-1}}$, and both have the same kernel in $P$. A nontrivial character of $P\cong C_3\times C_3$ maps onto $C_3$, so that kernel has order three. It acts trivially on all of $V$. Therefore **the group has no faithful irreducible complex representation**. In fact choosing nonzero $v\in V_\chi$ makes $\operatorname{span}(v,tv)$ invariant, so these nontrivial-character irreducibles have degree two.

= 20F
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Number Fields}

= Solution
{parent=20F}

Put $s=\sqrt{-17}$. The <ring of integers of a number field> is $\mathbb Z[s]$ and the <field discriminant> is $-68$. The <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> says that each <ideal class> has an integral ideal representative of norm at most $(2/\pi)\sqrt{68}<6$.

The prime two ramifies as $(2)=\mathfrak p_2^2$, where $\mathfrak p_2=(2,1+s)$. Three splits as $(3)=\mathfrak p_3\overline{\mathfrak p}_3$, choosing $\mathfrak p_3=(3,1+s)$, whereas five is inert because $-17\equiv3\pmod5$ is not a square. The norm bound therefore leaves only the classes of $1$, $\mathfrak p_2$, $\mathfrak p_3$ and $\overline{\mathfrak p}_3$; a norm-four ideal is $\mathfrak p_2^2$ and is principal.

The ideal $\mathfrak p_2$ is not principal, since an element of norm two would solve $a^2+17b^2=2$. On the other hand $N(1+s)=18$, and $1+s$ is divisible by $\mathfrak p_2$ and $\mathfrak p_3$ but not $\overline{\mathfrak p}_3$. Unique <prime ideal factorization> and the norm give $(1+s)=\mathfrak p_2\mathfrak p_3^2$. Thus $[\mathfrak p_3]^2=[\mathfrak p_2]$, which has order two, and
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-17}))\cong C_4.}
$$

For the Diophantine equation, $x$ must be positive. If $x$ were even, $y^2\equiv15\pmod{32}$, impossible for a square. Thus $x$ is odd and $y$ even. If $17\mid x$, then $17\mid y$, but $y^2+17$ would have 17-adic valuation one while $x^5$ has valuation divisible by five, also impossible.

The ideals $(y+s)$ and $(y-s)$ are consequently coprime. A common prime divides $2s$ and hence lies over two or seventeen, both excluded above. Their product is $(x)^5$, so unique <prime ideal factorization> gives $(y+s)=\mathfrak a^5$. Since the <ideal class group> has order four, $[\mathfrak a]^5=1$ forces $[\mathfrak a]=1$. Write $\mathfrak a=(a+bs)$ with integers $a,b$. The only units are $\pm1$ by their norm, and the sign can be absorbed into the fifth power. Hence $y+s=(a+bs)^5$.

Comparing the coefficient of $s$ gives
$$
1=b(5a^4-170a^2b^2+289b^4),\qquad b=\pm1.
$$
For $b=1$, the bracket would be one, but it is four modulo five. For $b=-1$, the bracket would be minus one, but it is one modulo three for either possible square residue of $a$. Both cases are impossible. Therefore **there are no integer solutions**.

= 21H
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= i
{parent=21h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**True.** A path $u:x\to y$ gives the basepoint-change isomorphism of <fundamental groups> $[\gamma]\mapsto[\overline u*\gamma*u]$. Concatenating paths is compatible with homotopy and loop multiplication. Using the reversed path gives its inverse, because $u*\overline u$ and $\overline u*u$ contract relative to endpoints.

= ii
{parent=21h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**False.** Changing the connecting path changes the isomorphism by an inner automorphism. Take $x=y$, one path constant and the other representing $a$ in the <Klein bottle group> $\langle a,b:aba^{-1}=b^{-1}\rangle$. The first induced isomorphism is the identity, whereas the second sends $b$ to $b^{-1}\ne b$. The element $b$ has infinite order, as is also witnessed by mapping it to a translation and $a$ to a reflection in the infinite dihedral group. A fixed path to a different endpoint can be appended if distinct basepoints are desired.

= iii
{parent=21h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**True.** Coordinate projections define $\pi_1(X\times Y,(x,y))\to\pi_1(X,x)\times\pi_1(Y,y)$. A pair of based loops gives the inverse by $t\mapsto(\gamma_X(t),\gamma_Y(t))$. Homotopies combine and project coordinatewise, and loop concatenation does too. These maps are well-defined inverse group homomorphisms, requiring no local connectedness assumptions.

= iv
{parent=21h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**False.** Take a bouquet of two circles and the two-dimensional torus. Both are connected polyhedra and have <first homology group> $\mathbb Z^2$. Their <fundamental groups> are respectively the nonabelian free group $F_2$ and the abelian group $\mathbb Z^2$, so they are not isomorphic. First homology only remembers the <abelianization> of the fundamental group.

= 22G
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=22G}

The real <Stone-Weierstrass theorem> says that a subalgebra of $C(X,\mathbb R)$ on a compact metric space is uniformly dense if it contains the constants and separates points. The finite sums $\sum_i f_i(x)g_i(y)$ form such an algebra on $[0,1]^2$: multiplication preserves this form, the constant one belongs to it, and the coordinate functions distinguish every pair of different points. Thus they approximate the continuous kernel uniformly.

A <finite-rank operator> has finite-dimensional range. A <compact operator> maps the unit ball to a set with compact closure, equivalently sends every bounded sequence to a sequence with a convergent subsequence. The identity on $C[0,1]$ is bounded but not compact: the functions $x^n$ have supremum norm one, and every subsequence has the discontinuous pointwise limit which is zero on $[0,1)$ and one at one. It therefore has no uniformly convergent subsequence.

A bounded finite-rank operator is compact because bounded sets in its finite-dimensional range have compact closure. Suppose $T_n\to T$ in <operator norm>. For any $\varepsilon>0$, choose $n$ with $\|T-T_n\|<\varepsilon/3$ and a finite $\varepsilon/3$-net for $T_n$ of the unit ball. The same centres give an $\varepsilon$-net for $T$ of that ball. Its closure is totally bounded and complete in the <Banach space> $Y$, hence compact. Thus **an operator-norm limit of compact operators is compact**.

For $K_n(x,y)=\sum_i f_i(x)g_i(y)$ uniformly approximating $K$, the integral operator $T_n$ has range in $\operatorname{span}\{f_i\}$ and is bounded. Moreover
$$
\|(T-T_n)f\|_\infty\leq\|K-K_n\|_\infty\|f\|_\infty.
$$
Consequently $T_n\to T$ in operator norm, proving that **the stated integral operator is compact**.

= 23G
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= Solution
{parent=23G}

Near an isolated point $P\in A$, choose source and target coordinate charts, using continuity of $\alpha$ to keep a sufficiently small source neighbourhood in the target chart. The coordinate representation is holomorphic on the punctured neighbourhood and is bounded near $P$ by continuity. The <Riemann removable singularity theorem> extends it holomorphically across $P$, with the given continuous value. Repeating at all points of $A$ proves that **$\alpha$ is analytic**.

For a nonconstant holomorphic function $f$ on a connected <Riemann surface>, let $A$ be the set where its coordinate derivative vanishes. This condition is independent of the chart. The derivative cannot vanish identically on an open set, because the <identity theorem> would then make $f$ constant globally. Its zeros are isolated and closed locally, so $A$ is a closed discrete subset. At every other point the <holomorphic inverse function theorem> makes $f$ a local coordinate chart.

Now suppose $\alpha$ is a homeomorphism and $g=f\circ\alpha$ is analytic. Outside $\alpha^{-1}(A)$, use $f$ as a target coordinate to write locally $\alpha=f^{-1}\circ g$. This is analytic. The preimage $\alpha^{-1}(A)$ is discrete because $\alpha$ is a homeomorphism, and the first argument extends analyticity across it. An injective holomorphic map cannot have a zero derivative: a zero of order at least two would give a locally multiple-to-one map. Its local inverse is therefore holomorphic, proving that **$\alpha$ is a conformal equivalence**.

The plane $\mathbb C$ and the unit disk are homeomorphic by $z\mapsto z/(1+|z|)$, with inverse $w\mapsto w/(1-|w|)$. They are not conformally equivalent: a holomorphic equivalence from the plane to the disk would be a bounded nonconstant <entire function>, contradicting <Liouville's theorem>.

= 24H
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Geometry}

= i
{parent=24h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

An <affine variety> is irreducible when it is nonempty and cannot be expressed as the union of two proper <Zariski closed sets>. If a nonempty open subset $U$ were not dense, $X=\overline U\cup(X\setminus U)$ would be such a union: the first set is proper by assumption, and the second is proper because $U$ is nonempty. Hence **every nonempty open subset of an irreducible variety is dense**.

= ii
{parent=24h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The coefficients of $\det(TI-A)$ are polynomial functions of the matrix entries. Its <polynomial discriminant> $\Delta(A)$ is therefore a polynomial in those entries, and it is nonzero exactly when all roots of the characteristic polynomial are distinct. Thus the required set is the <principal open subset> $D(\Delta)$ of affine space. It is nonempty: an algebraically closed field is infinite, so choose a diagonal matrix with $n$ pairwise distinct entries.

It is also affine as a variety. The map $A\mapsto(A,1/\Delta(A))$ identifies it with the closed hypersurface
$$
\boxed{\{(A,z):z\Delta(A)=1\}\subseteq\mathbb A^{n^2+1}.}
$$
Projection to $A$ is its regular inverse. This proves the requested affineness while retaining the open embedding into matrix space.

= iii
{parent=24h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Each entry of $\operatorname{char}_A(A)$ is a polynomial in the entries of $A$, so their simultaneous zero set $Z$ is <Zariski closed>. A matrix in $D(\Delta)$ has distinct eigenvalues and is diagonalizable. If $A=S\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)S^{-1}$, then
$$
\operatorname{char}_A(A)
=S\operatorname{diag}(\operatorname{char}_A(\lambda_1),\ldots,
\operatorname{char}_A(\lambda_n))S^{-1}=0.
$$
Thus $D(\Delta)\subseteq Z$. Affine space is irreducible because its coordinate ring is an integral domain; by part (i), its nonempty open subset $D(\Delta)$ is dense. The closed set $Z$ containing it must be all of affine space. This proves the <Cayley-Hamilton theorem>:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{char}_A(A)=0\quad\text{for every }A.}
$$

= 25I
{parent=Section II}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= Solution
{parent=25I}

A point $x$ is critical for a smooth map $f:X\to Y$ when $Df_x:T_xX\to T_{f(x)}Y$ is not surjective. A critical value is the image of a critical point; a <regular value> has no critical point in its fiber, including the case of an empty fiber.

To prove the <regular level set theorem>, take $x_0\in f^{-1}(y)$ and write $n=\dim X$, $m=\dim Y$. In local coordinates, surjectivity supplies an invertible $m\times m$ derivative minor; relabel source coordinates so it uses the first $m$ columns. The map
$$
\Phi(x)=(f_1(x),\ldots,f_m(x),x_{m+1},\ldots,x_n)
$$
has an invertible derivative. The <inverse function theorem> makes it a local diffeomorphism. In these coordinates the fiber is obtained by fixing its first $m$ coordinates, so it is locally an open subset of $\mathbb R^{n-m}$. These smooth charts prove that **a nonempty regular fiber is a smooth embedded manifold of dimension $n-m$**, with tangent space $\ker Df_x$.

For the <special orthogonal group>, use $F:M_n(\mathbb R)\to\operatorname{Sym}_n(\mathbb R)$ defined by $F(A)=AA^T$. Its derivative is $DF_A(H)=HA^T+AH^T$. At an orthogonal $A$, any symmetric matrix $B$ is obtained by taking $H=BA/2$, so $I$ is a regular value. Therefore $O(n)=F^{-1}(I)$ has dimension $n^2-n(n+1)/2=n(n-1)/2$. The determinant takes only the values $\pm1$ there, so $SO(n)$ is an open and closed part of $O(n)$ and has the same manifold dimension:
$$
\boxed{\dim SO(n)=\frac{n(n-1)}2.}
$$
It is closed in matrix space and bounded because every orthogonal matrix column has norm one. The <Heine-Borel theorem> proves compactness. Finally the tangent space is the derivative kernel,
$$
\boxed{T_ASO(n)=\{H:AH^T+HA^T=0\}
=\{AK:K^T=-K\}.}
$$
Thus its translated tangent space is the vector space of skew-symmetric matrices.

